WEBVTT

02:50.942 --> 02:52.944
Help me!

02:53.028 --> 02:55.447
Somebody, help me out.

02:56.615 --> 02:59.284
Somebody, help me over here!

03:08.293 --> 03:11.004
Somebody, help me out!

03:12.422 --> 03:14.132
Oh, my god!

03:28.313 --> 03:31.191
Walt! Walt!

03:31.274 --> 03:34.486
Stay away from the gas! Stay there!

03:44.162 --> 03:48.875
Help! Help! Somebody, help me!

03:56.842 --> 03:59.511
Oh, my leg!

04:03.682 --> 04:06.143
Hey, get over here.
Give me a hand.

04:06.226 --> 04:09.729
You, come on!
Come over here! Give me a hand!

04:09.813 --> 04:11.731
On the count of three.

04:11.815 --> 04:15.569
One, two, three!

04:31.501 --> 04:35.380
Help! Please, help me!

04:35.464 --> 04:37.799
Help me! Please, help me!

04:39.718 --> 04:42.596
Get him out of here.
Get him away from the engine.

04:42.679 --> 04:44.848
Get him out of here.

04:48.977 --> 04:50.979
Help me!

04:51.062 --> 04:53.648
Please, help me.
I'm having contractions!

04:53.732 --> 04:56.776
How many months pregnant are you?
— only eight months.

04:56.860 --> 05:00.280
How far apart are they coming?
— I don't know, a fewjust happened!

05:02.157 --> 05:05.035
Hey! Get away from there!

05:17.506 --> 05:20.425
Listen to me! Look at me!
You're gonna be ok.

05:20.509 --> 05:24.429
Do you understand me?
But you have to sit absolutely still.

05:28.183 --> 05:30.602
Hey! You! Come here!

05:30.685 --> 05:34.189
I need you to get this woman away
from these fumes. Take her there.

05:34.272 --> 05:38.193
Stay with her. If her contractions occur
closer than three minutes, call me!

05:38.276 --> 05:40.695
You gotta be kidding me.
— I'll be right back.

05:40.779 --> 05:42.864
Thank you.

05:42.948 --> 05:45.492
Hey! What's your name?

05:49.746 --> 05:53.542
Stop! Her head's not tilted back enough.
You're blowing into her stomach.

05:53.625 --> 05:58.213
You sure? That's exactly what I was
doing. I'm a lifeguard. I'm licensed.

05:58.296 --> 06:01.299
You need to think about
giving that license back.

06:01.383 --> 06:05.720
Maybe we should do one of those hole
things. Stick the pen in the throat?

06:05.804 --> 06:09.015
Yeah, good idea. You go get me a pen.

06:11.768 --> 06:14.729
Does anyone have any pens?
Do you have a pen?

06:14.813 --> 06:16.940
Does anybody have a pen?
— come on!

06:18.316 --> 06:20.068
Come on!

06:20.485 --> 06:23.613
Come on! Come on!

06:29.244 --> 06:31.204
Big deep breaths. Deep breaths.

06:47.596 --> 06:49.973
Move! Move! Move!

06:50.056 --> 06:52.017
Get her up! Get her out of there!

07:16.541 --> 07:19.628
You ok? — yeah. Yeah.

07:19.711 --> 07:21.796
You?

07:21.880 --> 07:25.425
Stay with her.
— dude, I'm not going anywhere.

08:15.350 --> 08:18.061
I didn't know
which one would work best.

08:25.276 --> 08:28.738
They're all good. Thanks.

10:07.629 --> 10:08.922
Excuse me.

10:11.800 --> 10:14.385
Did you ever use a needle?

10:15.345 --> 10:19.432
What?
— did you ever patch a pair of jeans?

10:25.897 --> 10:29.776
I made the drapes in my apartment.
— that's fantastic. Listen...

10:29.859 --> 10:33.238
Do you have a second?
I could use a little help here.

10:38.076 --> 10:40.245
Help with what?

10:40.328 --> 10:43.790
This. I'd do it myself,
I'm a doctor, but I just can't reach it.

10:43.873 --> 10:46.125
You want me to sew that?
— like the drapes.

10:46.209 --> 10:50.171
With drapes, I use a sewing machine.
— no, you can do this, I'm telling you.

10:50.255 --> 10:52.131
If you wouldn't mind.

10:59.430 --> 11:02.809
Of course I will. — thank you.

11:07.105 --> 11:09.482
It's for your hand.

11:12.110 --> 11:16.281
Save me some... for the wound.

11:21.327 --> 11:23.162
Any color preference?

11:25.123 --> 11:28.543
No. Standard black.

12:36.486 --> 12:38.571
Come on.

12:44.327 --> 12:46.329
Hey, you!

12:46.412 --> 12:49.457
What's your name? — me? Charlie.

12:49.540 --> 12:53.461
Charlie, we need help with the fire.
No one will see it if it isn't big.

12:53.544 --> 12:56.297
Ok, I'm on it. What's your name?
— Sayid.

12:56.381 --> 12:59.175
Sayid. I'm on it, Sayid.

13:10.395 --> 13:13.314
I might throw up on you.

13:13.398 --> 13:15.483
You're doing fine.

13:19.028 --> 13:21.030
You don't seem afraid at all.

13:22.407 --> 13:24.075
I don't understand that.

13:24.993 --> 13:28.705
Well, fear's sort of an odd thing.

13:32.458 --> 13:36.587
When I was in residency,
my first solo procedure

13:36.671 --> 13:42.051
was a spinal surgery
on a 16—year—old kid. A girl.

13:42.760 --> 13:48.391
And at the end, after 13 hours,
I was closing her up and I...

13:49.225 --> 13:51.978
I accidentally ripped her dural sack.

13:52.061 --> 13:55.440
It's at the base of the spine
where all the nerves come together.

13:55.523 --> 13:58.776
Membrane as thin as tissue, and...

13:58.860 --> 14:01.612
So it ripped open.

14:03.031 --> 14:07.243
Nerves just spilled
out of her like angel hair pasta,

14:07.326 --> 14:10.371
spinal fluid flowing
out of her and I...

14:16.210 --> 14:18.755
The terror was just so...

14:19.213 --> 14:23.468
Crazy, so real...

14:24.802 --> 14:27.805
And I knew I had to deal with it.

14:28.973 --> 14:32.060
So I just made a choice.

14:32.143 --> 14:36.189
I'd let the fear in.
Let it take over. Let it do its thing.

14:36.272 --> 14:40.068
But only for five seconds,
that's all I was gonna give it.

14:40.151 --> 14:42.361
So I started to count.

14:42.445 --> 14:45.406
One, two,

14:45.490 --> 14:50.369
three, four, five.

14:52.830 --> 14:54.457
And it was gone.

14:56.751 --> 14:59.879
I went back to work,
sewed her up and she was fine.

15:01.881 --> 15:06.135
If that had been me I think
I would've run for the door.

15:07.887 --> 15:10.681
No, I don't think that's true.

15:12.183 --> 15:14.852
You're not running now.

15:31.202 --> 15:33.621
You think they
would have come by now.

15:35.498 --> 15:38.668
Who? — anyone.

15:58.729 --> 16:01.566
As if I'm gonna start eating chocolate.

16:04.193 --> 16:08.656
Shannon, we may be here for a while.
— the plane had a black box, idiot.

16:08.739 --> 16:12.618
They know exactly where we are,
they're coming.

16:14.412 --> 16:17.456
I'll eat on the rescue boat.

16:17.540 --> 16:21.294
I'll eat on the rescue boat.

16:31.721 --> 16:35.933
Hungry? — yeah. Thanks.

16:37.518 --> 16:41.856
Any more... you know, baby stuff?

16:41.939 --> 16:45.902
No... I'm ok.

16:48.404 --> 16:53.618
Well, hang in there.
— yeah, you too.

17:05.296 --> 17:07.965
You sure you're warm enough?

17:48.089 --> 17:50.549
Do you think he's gonna live?

17:54.679 --> 17:57.056
Do you know him?

17:57.765 --> 18:00.768
He was sitting next to me.

18:08.150 --> 18:13.114
We must've been at about
40,000 feet when it happened.

18:13.197 --> 18:16.325
We hit an air pocket and dropped...

18:16.409 --> 18:19.704
Maybe 200 feet.

18:20.496 --> 18:23.040
The turbulence was...

18:31.716 --> 18:34.051
I didn't.

18:34.135 --> 18:36.595
I saw the whole thing.

18:40.641 --> 18:43.978
I knew that the tail was gone, but I...

18:44.061 --> 18:47.315
Couldn't bring myself to look back.

18:48.316 --> 18:52.278
And then the front end
of the plane broke off.

18:55.406 --> 18:58.075
Well, it's not here on the beach.

18:58.159 --> 18:59.535
Neither is the tail.

19:02.121 --> 19:04.332
We need to figure out
which way we came in.

19:04.415 --> 19:07.335
- Why?
- — there's a chance we find the cockpit.

19:07.418 --> 19:10.421
If it's intact, we might be able
to find the transceiver.

19:10.504 --> 19:13.549
We could send out a signal,
help the rescue party find us.

19:13.632 --> 19:16.135
How do you know all that?

19:17.219 --> 19:19.764
Took a couple of flying lessons.

19:21.599 --> 19:23.601
Wasn't for me.

19:26.604 --> 19:29.482
I saw some smoke...

19:29.565 --> 19:31.901
Just through the valley.

19:35.488 --> 19:39.116
If you're thinking about going
for the cockpit, I'm going with you.

19:47.666 --> 19:49.835
I don't know your name.

19:53.381 --> 19:55.383
I'm Kate.

20:13.984 --> 20:16.320
What was that?

20:19.698 --> 20:23.119
That was weird, right?
— Is that Vincent?

20:23.202 --> 20:25.287
It's not Vincent.

20:29.333 --> 20:31.460
Did anybody see that?

20:36.590 --> 20:38.717
Boone!

21:10.749 --> 21:13.085
Terrific.

21:28.809 --> 21:30.853
So, how's the drink?

21:32.688 --> 21:36.108
It's good.
— that wasn't a very strong reaction.

21:36.192 --> 21:39.069
Well, it's not a very strong drink.

21:43.073 --> 21:45.075
Just don't tell anyone.

21:45.159 --> 21:48.412
This, of course, breaks some
critical faa regulations.

22:19.235 --> 22:21.237
Excuse me.

22:21.862 --> 22:25.658
Sir, excuse me!
— guess he really had to go.

22:25.741 --> 22:27.952
Sir, excuse me!

22:39.922 --> 22:44.051
Ladies and gentleman, the pilot has
switched on the "fasten seatbelts" sign.

22:44.134 --> 22:47.721
Please return to your seats
and fasten your seatbelts.

22:47.805 --> 22:49.139
It's normal.

22:50.307 --> 22:54.853
Oh, I know. I've just
never been a very good flyer.

22:54.937 --> 22:59.400
My husband keeps reminding me
that planes want to be in the air.

22:59.483 --> 23:01.819
Well, he sounds like a very smart man.

23:01.902 --> 23:05.573
Be sure and tell him that
when he gets back from the bathroom.

23:10.661 --> 23:13.664
Well, I'll keep you company
until he does.

23:14.665 --> 23:18.210
Don't worry, it's gonna be over...

23:56.332 --> 23:59.043
It didn't sound like an animal,
not exactly.

23:59.126 --> 24:03.130
That sound it made, I keep thinking
there was something familiar about it.

24:03.213 --> 24:04.965
Where are you from? — the Bronx.

24:05.049 --> 24:08.010
It might be monkeys.
— It's monkeys. Monkey island.

24:08.093 --> 24:11.430
We don't know if we're on an island...
— you ready?

24:16.894 --> 24:19.855
You showed me where the smoke was.
I can get there myself.

24:19.938 --> 24:21.148
I'm coming.

24:24.443 --> 24:27.363
Well, you're gonna need better shoes.

25:27.923 --> 25:32.052
Whatever it was, it wasn't natural.

25:32.136 --> 25:36.265
Does anyone have any sunblock?
— yeah, I do.

25:36.807 --> 25:40.728
So I was just looking
inside the fuselage.

25:40.811 --> 25:43.063
It's pretty grim in there.

25:43.147 --> 25:46.692
You think we should
do something about the...

25:48.193 --> 25:50.237
B—o—d—y—s?

25:52.239 --> 25:55.409
What are you spelling, man, "bodies"?
— b-o-d-i-e-s.

25:55.492 --> 25:57.411
That sounds like a good idea.

25:57.494 --> 26:00.247
No, they'll deal with it
when they get here.

26:00.330 --> 26:02.541
I'm gonna go out
and look for the cockpit.

26:03.208 --> 26:07.504
See if we can find a transceiver to send
a distress signal. Help the rescue team.

26:07.588 --> 26:09.631
You need to keep an eye
on the wounded.

26:09.715 --> 26:12.301
If the guy in the suit wakes up,
keep him calm,

26:12.384 --> 26:15.095
but don't let him remove
that shrapnel. Understand?

26:15.179 --> 26:18.223
Got it. What about the guy
with the leg? The tourniquet...?

26:18.307 --> 26:20.768
I stopped the bleeding.
I took it off last night.

26:20.851 --> 26:25.230
He should be all right.
— yeah, cool. Good job.

26:25.314 --> 26:28.484
I'll come with. I wanna help.
— I don't need any more help.

26:28.567 --> 26:31.904
No, it's cool. I don't really feel
like standing still, so...

26:35.032 --> 26:36.325
Excellent.

26:42.623 --> 26:43.957
Can I ask you something?

26:44.041 --> 26:48.962
Me? I'd be thrilled. I've been waiting.

26:49.046 --> 26:51.715
Have we ever met anywhere?

26:52.216 --> 26:55.135
No. That would be unlikely.

26:56.220 --> 26:58.889
I look familiarthough, right?
— yeah.

27:00.015 --> 27:02.559
You can't quite place it?
— no, I can't.

27:02.643 --> 27:06.855
Yeah, I think I know. — you do?

27:06.939 --> 27:11.944
You all, everybody
you all, everybody

27:12.402 --> 27:14.488
never heard that song?
— I've heard it.

27:14.571 --> 27:17.324
I don't know what that...
— that's us! Drive shaft!

27:17.407 --> 27:20.619
The ring. Second tour of Finland.
Never heard of drive shaft?

27:20.702 --> 27:22.287
The band? — yeah, the band.

27:22.371 --> 27:25.624
You were in drive shaft?
— I am in drive shaft. I play bass.

27:25.707 --> 27:27.167
Serious? — yeah. Charlie.

27:27.251 --> 27:29.253
Track three. I do backing vocals.

27:29.336 --> 27:32.589
My friend Beth would freak.
— give me Beth's number, I'll call.

27:32.673 --> 27:35.467
She live nearby?
— you ever heard of drive shaft?

27:35.551 --> 27:37.219
You all, everybody

27:38.220 --> 27:40.180
you all, every... body

27:41.056 --> 27:43.100
we gotta keep moving.

27:44.601 --> 27:49.106
They were good.
— they are good. We're still together.

27:50.107 --> 27:52.776
We're in the middle of a comeback.

28:19.761 --> 28:22.431
You guys, is this normal?

28:23.140 --> 28:26.226
This kind of day turning into night.

28:26.310 --> 28:29.146
The—end—of—the—world type weather.
Guys?

28:42.159 --> 28:46.079
I'm telling you, you don't wanna go
in there! There's too many bodies!

29:05.474 --> 29:07.517
There it is again.

29:08.352 --> 29:09.978
Oh, my god.

29:55.524 --> 29:58.652
Well, let's do this.

30:37.232 --> 30:40.235
Let's get this trans...
— transceiver.

30:40.318 --> 30:42.946
Transceiver thing and get out of here.

31:45.008 --> 31:46.843
Come on!

31:52.974 --> 31:56.770
You ok? — yeah, you?

32:00.023 --> 32:04.444
I'm fine. Charlie's fine,
by the way. I'm ok.

32:06.947 --> 32:10.826
Hey, you don't have to come up here.
— no, I'm good.

32:32.430 --> 32:36.768
So, what does a transceiver look like?
— like a complicated walkie—talkie.

33:11.928 --> 33:14.681
Hey, can you hear me?

33:17.142 --> 33:18.268
I need that water.

33:24.774 --> 33:27.485
Here. Here you go.

33:32.032 --> 33:35.493
How many survived?
— at least 48.

33:36.244 --> 33:38.205
Does anything feel broken?
— no, no.

33:38.288 --> 33:42.626
Just my head's a little dizzy,
that's all.

33:42.709 --> 33:45.295
It's probably a concussion.

33:46.046 --> 33:49.007
How long has it been?
— sixteen hours.

33:49.090 --> 33:51.134
Sixteen?

33:53.637 --> 33:56.473
Has anybody come? — not yet.

34:00.268 --> 34:02.812
Six hours in...

34:05.023 --> 34:06.983
Our radio went out.

34:08.193 --> 34:09.819
No one could see us.

34:10.695 --> 34:13.698
We turned back to land in Fiji.

34:13.782 --> 34:17.160
By the time we hit turbulence...

34:18.495 --> 34:20.664
We were a thousand miles off course.

34:23.500 --> 34:27.003
They're looking for us
in the wrong place.

34:32.801 --> 34:35.387
We have a transceiver.
— good, good.

34:35.470 --> 34:37.597
We were hoping.
You shouldn't move.

34:37.681 --> 34:41.601
No, no. I'm ok.
Transceiver's in there.

34:41.685 --> 34:43.311
It's right there.

34:49.859 --> 34:52.279
Where's Charlie?

35:00.620 --> 35:02.038
It's not working.

35:14.759 --> 35:18.179
What were you doing in the bathroom?
— what?

35:24.227 --> 35:26.271
What the hell was that?

35:34.904 --> 35:38.158
It's right outside.
— what? What's right out...?

36:37.342 --> 36:38.927
What the hell just happened?

36:58.822 --> 37:01.032
Jack, come on!

37:01.116 --> 37:02.575
Just leave it!

37:59.591 --> 38:01.301
Ok, run!

38:52.894 --> 38:54.771
Fouh

38:56.814 --> 38:59.275
five.

39:17.961 --> 39:21.130
Where's Jack? Did you see him?
— I don't know. He pulled me up.

39:21.214 --> 39:23.341
Where is he? How can you?
— I don't know!

39:23.424 --> 39:26.803
We got separated. I fell down.
He came back. That thing was...

39:26.886 --> 39:29.514
Did you see it? — no. No.

39:29.597 --> 39:32.350
But it was right there.
We were dead... I was.

39:32.433 --> 39:36.479
And then Jack came back and he
pulled me up. I don't know where he is.

39:45.071 --> 39:48.950
We have to go back for him.
— go back? There?

39:49.033 --> 39:52.203
There's a certain
gargantuan quality about this thing.

39:52.620 --> 39:54.289
Then don't come.

40:27.614 --> 40:29.991
I heard you shout.

40:30.700 --> 40:33.286
I heard you shout "Jack."

40:33.369 --> 40:35.288
I'm Charlie, by the way.

40:39.208 --> 40:41.210
What is that?

41:06.152 --> 41:08.196
What is...? — it's the pilot.

41:16.788 --> 41:18.831
Did you see it?

41:18.915 --> 41:23.169
No. Itwas right behind me,
but I dove into the bushes.

41:23.920 --> 41:25.296
Guys...

41:25.380 --> 41:28.508
How does something like that happen?
